---
title: "Templates and presets"
sidebar:
  order: 11
---

Templates and presets are configuration, not project records. They determine
how NAHPU selects fields and renders output. For the surrounding data flows,
read [Export workflows](./data-export-workflows).

## Four concepts

| Concept | Controls | Persistent owner | Sharing path |
|---|---|---|---|
| Record export preset | Record type, field mappings, headers, repeated values | `record_export_presets` in `redb` | JSON, QR, or user-config transfer |
| Document template preset | Content and visual elements for one record block | `template_presets` in `redb` | Individual JSON or user-config transfer |
| Document layout preset | Page geometry, blocks, ordering, and pagination | `document_layouts` in `redb` | Individual JSON or user-config transfer |
| Current selection | Which preset/layout the device opens | `SharedPreferences` | Not portable configuration |

Bundled templates and layouts are shipped in app assets and seeded only when a
name does not already exist. They do not overwrite user edits. A suppressed
bundled template remains suppressed until the user restores it.

## Record export presets

`ExportPresetModel` is a versioned Dart model. The current record-export preset
schema is version 10. It stores:

- record type and specimen taxon selection;
- header format;
- ordered scalar, combined, list, and nested mappings;
- null fallbacks, case/text formatting, conditional replacements, and
  replacement rules;
- list and nested-record expansion modes;
- indexed-header style and separators.

Source fields use canonical `table::field` keys. A mapping such as
`[specimen::fieldNumber]` selects one field; an expression containing several
bracketed fields combines values with literal text. Nested mappings select
fields from repeated child namespaces such as `specimenPart` or `parasite`.

The persisted preset does not own the output filename, destination, or selected
CSV/TSV/Excel/JSON runtime format. Those belong to the export action.

When a field or table is renamed, preserve old presets with an explicit alias
or in-memory migration. Do not silently discard mappings or interpret an
unknown namespace as another table.

## Document templates

Templates are serialized `Template` models. They describe one printable block:
elements, positions, dimensions, colors, typography, images, placeholders,
conditional text, nested lists, and dynamic layout behavior.

`template_field_catalog.dart` derives the available field namespaces from the
record type and selected taxon. A template can therefore expose project,
personnel, site, event, specimen, taxonomy, coordinates, attributes, parts,
and related records without hard-coding every field in the editor.

`TemplateService` stores templates through the generated config bridge. An
individual JSON import is parsed before saving and must resolve a name conflict
explicitly. Exporting a template produces a reusable definition, not a copy of
the selected specimen data.

When adding a new placeholder, update the field catalog, value resolver,
formatting behavior, editor picker, compatibility aliases, and document tests.

## Document layouts

`DocumentLayoutService` stores the active layout model in `document_layouts`.
Layouts reference template names and store:

- page size or custom dimensions;
- orientation and page padding;
- template blocks, rows, columns, and counts;
- template padding and page breaks;
- sort field and direction;
- fill-page and continuous/tiled behavior.

Renaming or deleting a template must inspect layout usages. A layout that
references an unavailable template should be reported as incompatible rather
than silently rendered with a different template.

`DocumentPageSetupService` is an unreferenced legacy implementation. New work
must use `DocumentLayoutService` and the Rust `DocumentLayoutPreset` model.

## Configuration transfer

`nahpu_configs` keeps reproducibility-related values separate from SQLite.
Complete user-config export/import can select sections and replace them
atomically. Individual record-preset, template, and layout actions may have
different collision behavior, so document that behavior at the UI boundary.

Keep these versions separate:

- internal `redb` database schema;
- exported user-config schema;
- record-export preset schema;
- template/layout model compatibility;
- Rust crate release version.

Never use a crate version as a data migration marker.

## Change recipes

### Adding a record-export field

Update the source model/query, default export list, dynamic field-value map,
preset picker, header resolver, and any Darwin Core mapping. Add a preset
compatibility alias if the source key changes.

### Adding a template field or element

Update the field catalog, editor controls, resolver, formatting/conditional
logic, preview, and PDF writer tests. Check all record types and taxon filters.

### Adding a layout property

Update the Rust bridge model, config persistence, JSON import/export, editor,
preview, renderer, and compatibility behavior. Regenerate bindings after a
bridge-facing signature change.

### Adding a bundled default

Add the asset, seed only missing names, preserve suppressed names, and test
that existing user edits are not overwritten during startup or upgrade.

## Preset test checklist

- round-trip JSON serialization;
- supported and future schema versions;
- legacy source/table aliases;
- duplicate-name import behavior;
- missing template references in layouts;
- bundled default seeding and suppression;
- selected-section config replacement;
- preview/export parity;
- output with empty, long, repeated, and taxon-specific values.
